# 5. FIRESTORE DATA ISOLATION AND SECURITY AUDIT

This is one of the highest-priority areas.

The application uses business-scoped data, with a structure similar to:

businesses/{businessId}/...

Inspect the actual database structure and determine the real security boundaries.

Audit:

- firestore.rules
- Firestore collection structure
- Subcollections
- Server-side Firestore access
- Client-side Firestore access
- Business ownership
- Business membership
- Role-based access
- Read permissions
- Create permissions
- Update permissions
- Delete permissions

Test the following scenarios using Firebase Emulator or another safe isolated test environment:

### Scenario A — Authorized owner

An authenticated business owner should access their own business data according to the intended permissions.

### Scenario B — Unauthorized user

A user who does not belong to a business must not read or modify its data.

### Scenario C — Cross-business access

A user belonging to Business A must not access Business B by changing businessId in the URL, request body, query string, or Firestore path.

### Scenario D — Unauthorized role escalation

A regular user must not grant themselves owner or administrator privileges.

### Scenario E — Direct Firestore access

Attempt unauthorized access directly through the Firestore client SDK, not only through application API routes.

### Scenario F — Server-side authorization

Verify that backend endpoints independently enforce business membership and permissions.

Important:

Firestore Security Rules do not automatically protect privileged server-side Admin SDK operations.

Every server-side operation using privileged Firestore access must perform its own authorization checks.

Never assume that deployed Firestore rules protect server-side code.

### Security rules requirements

- Apply least-privilege access.
- Deny unauthorized access by default.
- Validate document ownership and business membership.
- Prevent unauthorized role changes.
- Validate important fields and data types.
- Protect sensitive documents.
- Avoid unnecessarily broad wildcard permissions.
- Ensure nested collections have appropriate rules.

If rules are incorrect, prepare corrected rules and test them in an emulator first.

Do not deploy them automatically.

